 "rulebook": "IFAB Laws of the Game 2025/26: Law 2 (a size-5 ball: 68-70 cm round, 410-450 g), Law 12 (handball: the hand or the arm), Law 13 (9.15 m from the ball until it is in play) and the glossary (a kick is a contact with the foot or the ankle). Receiving a pass is a skill, not a rule-book event: the pass and the control standard (first touch with a foot, the ball at rest within 1 m inside 3 s) are the benchmark's",
 "deviations": [
  "The pass is played by a two-wheel football machine standing on the grass 15.0 m away instead of a team-mate, the same in every attempt: its wheels drive the ball out 0.55 m above the grass, nearly level, at about 9.6 m/s with topspin (the two wheels give it at most about 35 rad/s, less than a rolling ball's): it skids and hops low along the grass and reaches the robot at about 6.0 m/s, as the family's former 8 m/s rolling pass did. The pitch is level and uniform (the official ball's rolling resistance, no bobble), no wind.",
  "Law 13's distance is applied to the robot itself: until the pass is played it stays 9.15 m from the ball machine, so that the pass is played, not met at the machine."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Trap the pass: take the football that is passed along the ground past you with a foot, and bring it to rest within a metre of you inside three seconds.",
  "rules": "A size-5 football (0.22 m across, 0.43 kg) waits in a ball machine 15.0 m ahead of you and 0.5 m to your right; about 3.2 s after the start the machine plays it along the ground at about 9.6 m/s, parallel to the way you face, so that it goes by 0.5 m to the right of where you stand. Your first touch must be with a foot or an ankle (the parts below the shin). After it you may play the ball as often as you like with any part of you except the hands and the arms. You succeed when, within 3.0 s of the first touch, the ball is under control: slower than 0.2 m/s for 0.5 s without a break, its centre all that time within 1.0 m of your pelvis (measured along the ground), you on your feet. You fail if the first part of you that the ball touches is not a foot or an ankle, if a hand or an arm touches the ball at any time (handball), if 3.0 s after the first touch the ball is not under control, if your pelvis comes within 9.15 m of the ball machine before the pass has left it, if you touch the machine or the waiting ball, if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the ground, or any part of you other than the feet on the ground), or after 10 s.",
  "venue": "You face +x on level grass. The ball machine stands 15 m ahead of you, its wheels half a metre to the right of the line you face along, aimed back along it. About 3.2 s after the start the pass leaves it; it reaches you about 2.0 s later, rolling at about 6.0 m/s, half a metre to the right of your pelvis (about 0.35 m outside your right foot).",
  "difficulty": "A foot has to be in the way of a 6 m/s ball at the right hundredth of a second, half a metre to the side, and it has to take the pace off: a ball that hits a planted foot bounces a few metres away, far outside the metre, and the walking policy cannot catch a ball that is faster than it."
 },
